</div>
<div class="player-stat mid">
<div class="player-stat-val">26.5"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Vertical Jump</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Nelson measured 6-foot-5 and 325 pounds at the 2018 combine, with 33¾-inch arms and a 10⅜-inch hand. He skipped the 40 but put up <strong>35 reps of 225 pounds</strong> on the bench, second among offensive linemen that year. He added a 26.5-inch vertical, an 8-foot-9 broad jump, a 4.62-second short shuttle (seventh among offensive linemen) and a 7.65 three-cone.</p>
<p>Those numbers describe exactly the guard he became: a very strong man who moves well in a phone booth. A guard rarely has to cover more than a few yards. What he needs is the strength to move a 300-pound defensive tackle off the ball and the short-area quickness to stay square in pass protection. The bench and shuttle capture both.</p>
<p>Indianapolis drafted him sixth overall, and he made the Pro Bowl in each of his first eight seasons, with first-team All-Pro honors in 2018, 2019 and 2020. Per Elias, only three other offensive players since the merger opened their careers with eight straight Pro Bowls: Franco Harris, Barry Sanders and Joe Thomas.</p>
<h3>Why Seattle wanted him</h3>
<p>The defending champions are 4-3 and ranked 21st in pass block win rate (46.8%) at the time of the deal, per ESPN. Right guard Anthony Bradford missed five games with a knee injury and left tackle Charles Cross is expected to miss time with an ankle injury. Nelson has only played left guard in the NFL, where Grey Zabel starts for Seattle, so how they'll line him up is still an open question. He's in the last year of his deal at a $16 million base salary.</p>
