</table></div>
<p>Side by side, the two profiles show how differently the two sides of the line are built. Nelson is heavier, shorter-armed and dominant in pure strength. Buckner is taller, longer and 34 pounds lighter, with better jumps and agility times. A guard needs to anchor and drive; a defensive tackle needs to get off the ball and into the backfield.</p>
<h3>Why San Francisco brought him back</h3>
<p>The 49ers are 6-1 but have been hit by injuries along the defensive line. Buckner, who has 4 sacks, 8 quarterback hits and 4 tackles for loss through seven games this season, gives them an experienced starter for a fourth-round pick. San Francisco will pay the roughly $7.9 million left on his 2026 salary, and he's set for free agency in March.</p>
